    description: 'At 10:30 p.m. on 29 October 1969, UCLA graduate student Charley Kline attempted to send the word "LOGIN" from a Sigma 7 computer here in Room 3420 of Boelter Hall to a machine at Stanford Research Institute 350 miles north. The system crashed after transmitting just "L" and "O." An hour later, the full message went through. This was the first transmission on ARPANET, the packet-switching network funded by the U.S. Department of Defense that became the backbone of the internet. The original Interface Message Processor — a refrigerator-sized Honeywell 516 — is now at the Computer History Museum in Mountain View.',
